{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner complete changed-byte assessment of R3 revision3 at4572724895ed4d6159eee1bcdd68e758d6143815. I read all530 lines of the main two-head diff, every changed byte against the previously fully read revision2, and the complete43-line corrections matrix. All3 file identities were independently checked; the723-line publication/authority facts are byte-identical to the previously fully read version. Main1216lines/82,181bytes SHA256a5718a32d1e7ca5bdb76b7fb9c272a3ae7d921b6a0572e1a7b7b8efae54bf98a; matrix13,589bytes SHA2564de9c6d45a45f821fd763d0517c621956bc40c5595e62206d2ea2aa9a91e07b6. Two-file whitespace passed. No source, tests, install, runtime or provider effect was executed.\n\nThe seven previous groups5fd10474 have substantial explicit repairs: autonomous hold credentials no longer require an open laptop; direct private authority is separate; hold/report/handover and full-incarnation checkpoint/fork identities are distinguished; pre-admission staging is honestly a real outside write with a ledger; founding now has pending/active phases instead of an alleged cross-object transaction; publication messages have exact operation/scope/incarnation identities and state mapping; check errors and qualified host assumptions are explicit; and new effects stop before settlement census without treating unrelated account resources as owned. These remain unadopted design choices. Five bounded corrections remain before filing.\n\n1. Make staging's permitted path agree with R2 and its own lifecycle. Section6.2 imposes a held hold under a commitment for both report and propose-manifest, in the lane that stages. R2 revision4 section4.1 has11 change item types and no hold or commitment; its propose-manifest row names proposal/current manifest and permits a one-person unplanned change with no selected report. I reread the complete current item table and that act row. The new common precheck cannot simply demand a local change-lane hold that the chosen definition cannot contain. Specify separately the authorized report source and the integration/new-manifest source: exact owning lane/hold/instance, creation/computation ownership and permitted transfer. If a foreign work hold is intended, state its verified reference and the local decision/current authority path; if change itself must support work holds, reconcile its forms/counts and browser flow with R2. Preserve one-person changes and full integration work; don't cure this by requiring an unexplained extra issue/commitment or weakening the authorization.\n\nAlso distinguish already staged immutable commits from newly required staging. Section5.2 explicitly allows the same performer to report an already staged commit after hold end, while6.2's precheck always requires a held hold before staging. State that the earlier completed stage can be reused without new mint/copy, subject to actual report authority, pins and intent/reference checks. Handover still prevents the old performer reporting. Qualify the sentence 'a refused, expired or revoked proposer therefore causes no write' to rejection before dispatch. A precheck passing followed by revocation, expiry, moved state or refusal at actual admission can leave a real staging/mint duty; no zero-effect claim follows. R1's selected preparation rule and fresh commit validation remain pending, not a distributed atomicity claim.\n\n2. Give the staged-ref owner set a deletion fence. Section6.2's owner list and expected-ref-value delete are useful, but expected Git value alone cannot serialize new owners with a queued/in-flight delete. Concrete case: last owner drops; deleteD is sent with expected commitC and its answer is lost; another operation requires the same lane/incarnation/commit staging name and creates/reuses it atC; lateD can satisfy the same value check and remove the new owner's only root. This is possible even with one legitimate staging owner, not an unrelated-writer attack. Define pin acquisition/release and delete dispatch/reconciliation on the owning ledger with an explicit retirement state or unique nonreused root incarnation; no new owner may rely on a root while a deletion could still apply. A recreated name must not expose the same-value ABA race.\n\nState how an input or manifest in a different lane registers and retains its pin before its contribution becomes usable, and what authoritatively releases it. Local cached owner knowledge or an empty list before a delayed cross-scope message is insufficient. Shared-root cleanup cannot delete accepted reports or pending integrations, and an unknown deletion retains its own attempt/credential/reconciliation owner. One two-order pin/delete table plus lost-answer case is enough design evidence; no new provider sweep or legacy schema is required. Preserve replay-retention and all required Git object closure separately.\n\n3. Make founding's 'done once' recovery implementable under the ledger rule. Section3.8's crash row says retry of a pending claim makes repository creation and genesis each done once. Section5.4 correctly says occupying a name or inventory does not answer an unknown create, and a retry is another step. Specify the actual creation duty, dispatch identity and authenticated completion/fence or supported idempotency contract. A deterministic name and an already-exists refusal avoid some duplicate physical names but do not alone identify ownership, return creation credentials, or settle the earlier request. Preserve the unknown duty, refuse adoption of an unrelated existing repository, and explain whether recovery can safely continue while that duty remains unsettled under a separately justified bound. Do not replace this with an unsupported exactly-once claim.\n\nFor the directory-result/active-claim step, specify exact claim, genesis incarnation and result correlation so a late result cannot activate another incarnation. Keep genesis/bootstrap actions fenced from ordinary writes until activation, and retain R1's provisional-creation confirmation requirements. If the register is missing, the stated operator incident must actually fence new founding rather than merely label an empty state while the six-step flow continues. These are design rules, not permission for a reset, host call or old-format support.\n\n4. Fix the fence's observer result and receipt table in both orders. Section6.8 now correctly makes the original published and the fence aborted when the integration commit won; when the fence won it makes the original aborted and the fence published. The same table still unconditionally says 'P's merge is aborted' and 'a receipt for the fence'. Those contradict its integration-winner row and6.4's published-to-published mapping. Make P's merge/proposal state, incident text, receipt duty and actual published commit conditional on the winner. The losing fence does not owe a publication receipt as though it landed. Unknown attempts and token duties remain, including after a final operation. The already qualified H1/H2/H4 assumptions remain operational assumptions; no finite race witness is promoted to a universal guarantee.\n\n5. Specify the effective private-access boundary. Section4.2 checks a private stream every60seconds with an observation at most10seconds old. State the resulting revocation bound, including observation age and bounded check completion/failure, rather than implying that the direct-read10second freshness is itself a stream termination bound. Public read-session expiry is the separately chosen at-most10minutes; none of these authorize a control or tool. Stale/unavailable authority must close the owned stream under the chosen bound and not leave a pending recheck open forever.\n\nFor export, the row says copy into the receiving hold's environment and recheck authorization before the receiver is given the checkpoint. Define when checkpoint bytes become visible to that environment's runner/holder. Copying into an already readable destination filesystem can give it bytes before that final check. Use an inaccessible transfer stage/release boundary, or explicitly choose and disclose the first-authorized-copy irreversible boundary and its revocation effects. Do not claim a later failed check recalls bytes already delivered. Preserve exact export identity, lost-result deduplication, source/receiver pins and current authorizer/recipient rules; no change silently exports private old-holder work.\n\nR1 current447c7846 identity/relationship/timed-drain findings remain dependencies, and R2 current84cda1ba owns its missing error/timeout forms and durable browser dispatch fence. R4 complete-audit correction97528419 already addresses the actual corresponding witnesses; fold these precise paths into its compact proposal rather than repeating whole-platform gates. The browser/durable/device stories, split/join/qualified review, public/cold-person/manual/replay/deployed acceptance and all-component removal scope are unchanged. The exact staging/forming/founding choices, actual effect settlement and removal tasks still need reviewed adoption and owned implementation. This is internal changes requested on live original R3 request/promise and Hugh'sd4e07064, not independent approval or a new source commission. No compatibility path, task restart, provider action, numerical quota or fixed Jam gate is introduced. Please revise the note/matrix before ordinary independent filing when the sprint owner releases it.","body":{"head":"4572724895ed4d6159eee1bcdd68e758d6143815","scope":"R3 revision3 complete changed-byte assessment; design only","verdict":"internal-changes-requested"}}